WEBVTT

00:00:16.239 --> 00:00:19.680
Speaker 1: Now, there are four classical arguments for the existence of God in nature.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.480
Speaker 1: Well, I wouldn't say in nature generally.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:28.950
Speaker 1: Four classic arguments that everyone who is interested in apologetics must know.

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:30.070
Speaker 1: Right.

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:30.310
Speaker 1: If.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:37.830
Speaker 1: If this is a topic that's dear to your heart, which it should because of the reasons we gave you, they matter that you know them.

00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:41.670
Speaker 1: And the first one is the cosmological argument.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:46.550
Speaker 1: The cosmological argument, and it's very simple.

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:52.940
Speaker 1: It states that whatever begins to exist, that is, whatever has a beginning, has a cause.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:53.700
Speaker 1: I'll take it again.

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:56.100
Speaker 1: Whatever begins to exist.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:00.420
Speaker 1: Whatever has a beginning, has a cause.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:10.020
Speaker 1: And so that follows by saying, if the universe exists, it means it has a cause that is different from the universe itself.

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:11.700
Speaker 1: Did it have a start?

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:14.340
Speaker 1: Did it begin to exist at some point?

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:17.220
Speaker 1: If it did, then something must have caused it.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:18.430
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:22.430
Speaker 1: And somebody would say, well, yeah, you're right.

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:24.030
Speaker 1: But it's a big Bang.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:28.990
Speaker 1: Some elements bang together and then universe was created.

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:30.910
Speaker 1: That's great, that's good.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:35.950
Speaker 1: But the things that bang together, where did they, where did they come from?

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:39.310
Speaker 1: The things that made those things come from, where did they come from?

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:39.990
Speaker 2: Exactly.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:44.630
Speaker 1: So you must always be able to trace it back to a source.

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:47.910
Speaker 1: And this is the cosmological argument.

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:48.550
Speaker 2: Yeah.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:52.470
Speaker 2: And I think that's where the Big Bang theory sort of fails.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:55.070
Speaker 2: But then I think it also begs the question, passive, right?

00:01:55.070 --> 00:02:04.550
Speaker 2: Because if I was applying this cosmological argument to say, a ball, I see a ball rolling, then I would ask who kicked the ball?

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:05.190
Speaker 2: Right.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:07.790
Speaker 2: Then I would ask what made the person kick the ball.

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:13.000
Speaker 2: So it's almost imposes this infinite regression Right.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:17.320
Speaker 2: Of different causalities just to making the actual causation.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:18.560
Speaker 1: So one ball hits one ball.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.000
Speaker 1: What caused that ball to hit the ball?

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:21.880
Speaker 1: What caused the previous ball to hit the other ball?

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:22.520
Speaker 2: Exactly.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.120
Speaker 1: So what caused what caused what?

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:25.960
Speaker 1: Keeps going back.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:31.000
Speaker 1: And so the argument is, well, it is not possible to have it continuing infinitely.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:31.520
Speaker 2: Infinitely.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:32.440
Speaker 1: Yeah, absolutely.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:44.050
Speaker 1: So the cosmological argument states that while there are things that may have caused things, you will always go back to one initial cause.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:46.930
Speaker 1: There is always a first start.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:49.810
Speaker 1: There will always be a first start.

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:55.250
Speaker 1: And so it means that this first start must itself not have had a cause.

00:02:55.730 --> 00:03:02.370
Speaker 1: So it must be timeless, it must be uncaused, it must be changeless, and it definitely must be immaterial.

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:04.070
Speaker 1: So it must be uncaused.

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:08.830
Speaker 1: Means if something caused it, then that means itself is not the first cause.

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:11.310
Speaker 1: There must be a first uncaused cause.

00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:13.390
Speaker 1: Okay, it is timeless.

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:16.830
Speaker 1: Means it existed outside of time.

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:26.030
Speaker 1: Everything that is caused exists within time because at some point it didn't exist, then at a particular time it came to be, it was caused to be.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:29.940
Speaker 1: So the first cause must be outside of time.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:30.340
Speaker 2: Right.

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:33.300
Speaker 1: It must be changeless and it must be immaterial.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:38.260
Speaker 1: So these four attributes are attributes that help you understand.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:42.660
Speaker 1: You address the whole question of that infinite regress.

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:54.260
Speaker 1: And if I was to ask you, who is it or what is it that is timeless, changeless, immaterial and on course there is only one answer and it's a three letter word.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:56.100
Speaker 1: God.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:57.760
Speaker 1: All right, yes.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.320
Speaker 1: Colossians chapter one, verse 16.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:02.640
Speaker 1: It says, for by Him.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:06.040
Speaker 1: Now I'm saying all these things and I'm backing them up.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:11.040
Speaker 1: Well, we are backing them up with scriptures, just so you understand that our arguments are not baseless.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:12.280
Speaker 1: Okay?

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:19.240
Speaker 1: Colossians 1:16 says, for by him all things were created that are in heaven.

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:23.610
Speaker 1: He's telling you that these uncaused cause.

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:25.850
Speaker 1: He's telling you who it is.

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:35.290
Speaker 1: For by him all things were created that are in heaven, that are on the earth, visible and invisible, whether thrones or dominions or principalities or powers.

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:39.010
Speaker 1: All things were created through him as so powerful.

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:40.650
Speaker 1: I feel it's there even as I say it.

00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:48.850
Speaker 1: Created through him and for him, he is the beginning of all things, and in him all things consistent.

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:51.880
Speaker 1: Pinch yourself a little bit and just say, thank you Jesus.

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:52.680
Speaker 2: Thank you, Jesus.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:53.720
Speaker 1: It's so powerful.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:54.280
Speaker 2: Yeah.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:54.760
Speaker 1: Right.

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:58.040
Speaker 1: So this is God, the uncaused cause.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:04:58.600
Speaker 2: Yeah.

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:08.280
Speaker 2: Now sometimes people would also argue that, you know, quantum physics proves that nothing or something can come out of nothing.

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:25.720
Speaker 2: But I find that's a very misrepresentation or a misunderstanding of nothingness or what nothing means, because for in quantum physics, and again, those different materials, gases that are working together, it's nothing.

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:26.120
Speaker 2: Right?

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:46.880
Speaker 2: Because if you, if scientists should look at it in depth when they splice it in a very micro way, you realize that what you're calling as nothing, or what you're calling as emptiness is actually multiple gases, multiple molecules working together to produce something.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:50.720
Speaker 1: The beautiful thing about this is just to make it very simple.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:54.320
Speaker 1: Oftentimes, I don't know if you played with science experiments.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:06:00.960
Speaker 1: Sometimes I scroll on Instagram and some people are trying to show you what an object would be under a microscope.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:06.560
Speaker 1: And you can maybe wave your hand this way and you say there's nothing in your hand.

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:12.400
Speaker 1: But by the time you put your hand under a microscope, you see all the moving organisms, all the germs.

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:13.120
Speaker 2: Yeah.

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:15.840
Speaker 1: That are on your hand, your face.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:16.240
Speaker 1: Right.

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:17.880
Speaker 1: So this is not.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:22.560
Speaker 1: There's nothing on my hand, but it's different from absolute nothingness.

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:23.040
Speaker 2: Right.

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:26.560
Speaker 1: There is nothing on my hand, but it is not absolute nothingness.

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:29.880
Speaker 1: So when they say, oh, well, there was nothing.

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:30.680
Speaker 1: We didn't see anything.

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:32.000
Speaker 1: And then things came together, boom.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:36.240
Speaker 1: There was something there that you may have not perceived, you may have not seen.

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:40.320
Speaker 1: And so even in quantum physics, the concept of nothing has to be clarified.

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:40.860
Speaker 2: Yeah.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:43.700
Speaker 1: The nothing they refer to is not absolute nothing.

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:44.740
Speaker 2: Yep.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:45.300
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:45.780
Speaker 2: Yeah.

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:50.020
Speaker 1: So there is no rule to say, oh, it's possible that something came out of nothing.

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:55.860
Speaker 1: The only way something can come out of nothing is if someone made it.

00:06:57.780 --> 00:06:58.860
Speaker 1: That's the argument.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:01.100
Speaker 1: That's the cosmological argument.

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:03.380
Speaker 1: Okay, should we move on to the next one?

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:05.780
Speaker 1: The next one is a theological.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:07.480
